<title>BELGIUM: EU raises rice import quotas from British territories.</title>
<headline>EU raises rice import quotas from British territories.</headline>
<dateline>BRUSSELS 1997-02-12</dateline>
<text>
<p>The European Union agreed to increase duty free quotas for imports of rice from Montserrat and the Turks and Caicos islands by 8,000 tonnes during the first four months of 1997, British officials said on Wednesday.</p>
<p>Agreement was reached by qualified majority in COREPER, they said.</p>
<p>Montserrat, which has been devastated by volcanic eruptions, was granted an extra 4,594 tonnes and the Turks and Caicos islands an extra 1,328 tonnes.</p>
<p>Britain last month called for higher quotas to ensure the survival of the rice industry in its Caribbean overseas territories which it said were crippled by EU safeguard measures.</p>
<p>The EU, in response to Italian and Spanish complaints of a sharp rise in cheap rice imports, limited the first Jan/Apr tranche of an annual tariff freee quota for overseas countries and territories (OCTs) to 42,650 tonnes.</p>
<p>The action was taken after rice imports from OCTs rose to 365,029 tonnes in 1996, from 58,000 tonnes in 1992.</p>
</text>
